to easily visualize the results  there are many tools that integrate with  bigquery including google sheets and  data studio  at shopify we use mode analytics for  data analysis  in mode we build our sql queries  separately and then we can access the  data sets by name in a notebook  plotting our query results is pretty  trivial here are the plots from our  earlier queries  and from a similar query for request  rate to our core monolith  to calculate duration as a distribution  we bucketize by a minute and compute  each percentile over each minute  we represent duration in microseconds so  here i'm converting to milliseconds  and here's what that looks like as a  plot before we drill in further  one more high level perspective we might  like is the relationship between the  services involved  and the relative volume of traffic this  is variously called a service graph  or service map here's what that looks  like in this example  the key pieces we need to know are the  source destination and request count for  each edge in the graph  the query required for this is a little  more complicated  but there's really two things going on  here the first part of this query  considers requests that are represented  by a single span  these are typically database or cache  queries where we only have the  instrumentation on the client side  the other set of requests we're  interested in have two sides  so we grab the set of time spans with  the tracing span ids and the set of  server stands with the tracing parents  fan ids  and then join them on the trace id where  the parent span  id of the server is the client span id  grouping gives us the request count  along each edge  so let's go deeper and break down  request counts and durations by  component  for request count we query the rails  controller attribute as well  and group by both start time and the  controller in some cases the attribute  is missing so we replace it with unknown  plotting this gives us a request rate  for the different controllers  breaking down the duration by controller  is slightly more gnarly  i built this query in the bigquery  console by trial and error and a data  analyst somewhere is probably having a  fit watching me try to play one on tv  regardless our sub query extracts the  start time controller and duration from  the relevant stands  and then we compute the p95 partition by  the start time and controller  the result is a little obscure by an  outlier controller which looks like the  not found controller  we could ignore this controller in the  sql query but it's easier to do it here  with pandas  this produces a much clearer breakdown  drill downs are also relatively easy  with padders  here we've drilled down into the  collections controller we can then  easily contrast the duration for this  controller  with the request rate for the controller  from our earlier query  now we might want to ask where we're  spending time in aggregate in request to  this controller  let's start by querying all the  operations performed during those  requests  we have 26 operations which is quite  manageable  we have the expected redis and mysql  queries and template parsing and  rendering  computing the workload contribution of  each operation is as simple as summing  the duration and grouping by the  spamming  ordering by the cumulative duration  allows us to quickly identify the heavy  hitters  the top two aren't interesting but the  rest are leaf spans and we can clearly  see my sql dominates followed by redis  we can further summarize my database  type to see the cumulative my sequel and  radius times  in this service we also tag spans with  the time spent in garbage collection  which we can then extract and summarize  we're interested to know whether gc  dominates any of the expensive  operations  in fact it doesn't it accounts for less  than one percent of time spent in my sql  spans  and a little over four percent of  overall request time  alternatively we can look at the  contribution of different sql queries  in this service we tag my sql query  spans with a digest of the query  template  which makes it easy for us to aggregate  spans and then we can search for  examples and find the actual queries in  another tag  here we see one query clearly dominates  finally in the same way we did with  controllers  we can analyze request rate and duration  by arbitrary tags  this is a request count by the zone  and this is a p95 duration by zone we  can see some small differences but  nothing especially exciting  if we instead look at cash hit versus  missed performance we can see a more  distinct difference  and we can see that our cash hit rate is  reasonable  at over 96 i hope that's given you a  taste of some of the types of analyses  that you can perform over trace data  using bigquery the key elements are  getting trace data  into bigquery which you can accomplish  with cloud trace export  then extracting interesting traces to a  separate table  typically based on a time window this  helps to control query costs  and greatly increases responsiveness and  finally  it's extremely helpful to have a tool to  view the query results  at shopify we use mode analytics but  there are many other tools that  integrate with bigquery  thank you francis and thank you all for  joining  please feel free to reach out for  follow-up and as always  visit us at cloud.google.com
 

